QEMU device models for the Raspberry Pi 3B port
===============================================

Patches against qemu v10.1.3, the version these boards are tested on.
They are here because parts of the Pi 3B are either unmodelled by QEMU
or modelled in a way that cannot exercise the guest driver: without
them the corresponding kernel code is written blind.

To apply and build:

	git clone --depth 1 --branch v10.1.3 \
		https://gitlab.com/qemu-project/qemu.git
	cd qemu && git am /path/to/tools/qemu-patches/*.patch
	mkdir build && cd build
	../configure --target-list=aarch64-softmmu --disable-docs \
		--disable-fuse --disable-jack
	ninja

Run the result by explicit path; do not install over a packaged qemu.
On macOS the binary is qemu-system-aarch64-unsigned, which runs fine
under TCG.

EVIDENCE TIERS.  Results in doc/raspi3b-completion-plan.ms are labelled
by what they were checked against, strongest first:

	hardware	a real Raspberry Pi 3B
	qemu		a device model QEMU already shipped
	qemu+patch	a device model in this directory

qemu+patch is the weakest of the three and is called out wherever it is
used.  Both halves are ours there, so a passing test can mean the model
implements the driver's bug.  Two rules keep that honest, and they are
worth keeping when adding to this series:

  - write the model from the hardware documentation or an existing
    Linux/9front driver, not from what the Inferno driver happens to
    emit, and say in the patch where it deviates;

  - make the model reject protocol violations rather than tolerate
    them.  QEMU's own usb-msd answering "Bad CBW size" is what caught
    the dropped bulk maxpkt in appl/cmd/usb/usbd.b; a permissive model
    would have hidden it.

0017 sd: refuse a data transfer whose two ends disagree about bus width.
	Adds sdbus_set_bus_width(), mirroring sdbus_set_voltage(), so the
	host's width reaches the card; sd.c compares it against ACMD6's
	sd_status[0] at both data-transfer entry points and refuses with a
	LOG_GUEST_ERROR naming both widths.  Refusing rather than garbling
	on purpose: real hardware returns whatever the undriven lines hold
	and the corruption surfaces later with nothing pointing at it, and
	a model reproducing that faithfully would only reproduce the part
	that makes the bug hard to find.  The hook is on sd_class_init and
	not the common class, since eMMC carries bus width in EXT_CSD and
	never issues ACMD6.

0018 hw/char/pl011: model a transmit FIFO, opt-in per board.
	Adds tx-fifo-depth and tx-drain-bps properties; at the default
	depth of zero the device is byte-identical to before, so no other
	PL011 board's timing moves.  Set, it models a real FIFO drained a
	character at a time by a timer, asserting FR.TXFF when full and
	raising the transmit interrupt as room appears.  This is the entry
	below that said the gap was real but the cost too high; making the
	behaviour opt-in per board is what removed the cost.  bcm2835 asks
	for the 16x8 FIFO the datasheet gives UART0, paced at 3 Mbaud --
	the fastest the part can be set to, so the throughput cost is the
	least of any rate the hardware could really run at.  tx-drain-bps
	has no default and realize() refuses a depth without one: a guest
	that never programs IBRD leaves the model no baud rate to pace
	against, and a rate invented inside the device would be a number
	nobody could trace back to anything.

0019 hw/misc/bcm2835_property: report the UART clock that is actually
	wired.  GET_CLOCK_RATE answered a 3 MHz constant for the UART
	while bcm2835_peripherals drives the PL011 from the CPRMAN's
	uart-out at 47999625 Hz -- the model told a guest one number and
	ran at another, sixteen times apart.  UARTCLK is not a property
	of the part, so a driver has to ask; having asked, it computes
	IBRD/FBRD from 3 MHz and pl011_get_baudrate() divides 48 MHz by
	them.  An Inferno kernel asking for 115200 got 1846136, with
	nothing in the guest able to detect it.  The clock input is
	optional, so an unwired board keeps the constant.

0020 hw/usb/hcd-dwc2: stop the GHWCFG2 queue depths overflowing their
	fields.  The two transmit queue depths are 2-bit fields and both
	were written as a raw 4, which needs three bits, so each set a
	bit in the field above it.  A guest read back 0x250dc016 --
	non-periodic depth 0, periodic depth 1, 9 device tokens -- where
	the source says 4, 4 and 8.  Nothing in the model reads these
	registers back; they exist solely for the guest, which is why it
	survived.  The fields now go through a HWCFG() macro that fails
	the build if a value does not fit the field it names.

0021 hw/arm/bcm2835_peripherals: give every BCM SoC the blocks a
	BCM2711 has.  PWM, I2S, SMI, the BSC slave, the ARM timer and
	the MPHI mapping were realized only by the Pi 2/3 realize, so
	raspi4b had holes in its peripheral window where a BCM2711 has
	silicon -- and a guest driving one took a synchronous external
	abort rather than reading zeroes, which is how the set was
	enumerated.  The five device fields move to the base state and
	their realize to the common function, after the I2C controllers
	since the BSC slave sits on BSC0's bus.  Their interrupts still
	go to the legacy controller, which on a Pi 4 is not wired to the
	GIC, so they are reachable by polling and not by interrupt; that
	gap is recorded and not guessed at.  The RNG and the thermal
	sensor deliberately do not move: both are different blocks on a
	BCM2711, so moving them would assert something untrue about the
	hardware in exchange for a marker.

0022 hw/gpio/bcm2838_gpio: put the SD card on emmc2, where a Pi 4 has
	it.  bcm2838 mapped emmc2, gave it a capabilities register and
	or-gated its interrupt, then parented the card to the Arasan and
	never to emmc2 -- a controller fully present in the model and
	permanently empty.  A BCM2711 wires its microSD slot to emmc2 on
	dedicated pins; it is what the board boots from and what Linux's
	bcm2711.dtsi carries the card on.  Putting it on the Arasan is
	worse than leaving emmc2 empty, because a driver written against
	the model passes every check and then finds nothing at all on
	hardware, which is what this port's did.  The GPIO 48-53 mux
	stays and now moves nothing, since sdbus_reparent_card() returns
	at once for a bus with no card.

0023 hw/misc: model the Broadcom RNG200, and give the Pi 4 the one it
	has.  A BCM2711's RNG is an RNG200, not the BCM2835 block; qemu
	modelled the RNG200 for no machine at all, mapped the BCM2835
	one only from the Pi 2/3 realize, and raspi4b.c nops the
	brcm,bcm2711-rng200 node out of the device tree "until
	implemented".  The register map and the bring-up sequence come
	from drivers/char/hw_random/iproc-rng200.c, the driver Linux
	uses for the block; registers it does not name are not
	modelled.  The FIFO is the point: unlike bcm2835_rng, which
	hands out a word on any read of its data register whatever the
	guest did to RNG_CTRL, this gives nothing until the generator
	is enabled and complains to the guest-error log if the data
	register is read empty -- so a driver that skips the documented
	sequence hears about it here rather than on the board.  Two
	behaviours are inference rather than citation and say so in the
	source; the interrupt (GIC SPI 125) is deliberately not driven,
	because the published interface has status bits and no enable.
	The 0x28 window the model claims is the one bcm2711.dtsi gives
	the block: rng@7e104000, reg = <0x7e104000 0x28>.

0024 hw/misc: model the BCM2711's temperature sensor, in the AVS
	monitor.  A BCM2711's die sensor is not the BCM2835 one at
	another address: it is a different block with one register
	instead of two, a different calibration, and it is not in the
	peripheral window at all -- it is in the AVS monitor at VC
	0x7d5d2000, which the ARM sees at 0xfd5d2000, in the lower
	peripheral range this SoC maps and nothing had been placed in.
	Both halves are cited: bcm2711.dtsi has avs-monitor@7d5d2000
	with reg = <0x7d5d2000 0xf00> and coefficients = <(-487)
	410040>, and drivers/thermal/broadcom/bcm2711_thermal.c reads
	AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS at 0x200, requires bits 16 and 10, and takes
	the data from bits 9:0.  The whole window is claimed and only
	that register implemented.  Unlike bcm2835_thermal, which
	hardcodes 25C, the temperature is a property -- one qdev line,
	and the only way to tell a driver that decodes the reading from
	one that returns a constant.

WHAT COULD GO UPSTREAM.  Two of these are plain qemu bugs, independent
of Inferno and of this port, and could be sent to qemu-devel on their
own:

	0006	cprman matched the wrong mux's divisor register.
		Affects every clock mux, so every guest.
	0008	the mini UART dropped its storage registers, so a
		driver could not read back what it had written.
	0019	the firmware's GET_CLOCK_RATE contradicted the clock
		wired to the PL011 by a factor of sixteen.  Affects
		any guest that programs its baud divisors from what
		the firmware tells it, which is the only correct way
		to get UARTCLK.
	0020	three GHWCFG2 fields in the DWC2 host controller were
		wrong, two of them because a neighbouring field
		overflowed into them.  Affects any guest that reads
		the core's own description of itself, which is how a
		DWC2 driver is supposed to configure.

0010 is a bug in 0009, not in qemu; it is kept as a separate patch
because the reasoning is worth reading.  Everything else adds a device
model qemu does not have, and only matters to someone emulating a Pi in
this much detail \- which is most of the series, and increasingly so as
it grows.  Do not read the section above as a to-do list for upstream:
four patches of twenty-four are general-purpose fixes and the rest are not.

WHAT HAS BEEN CROSS-CHECKED.  Several of these models were written
from the datasheet and from recollection of a Linux driver, which is
worth exactly as much as the recollection.  They have since been
checked, register by register, against Linux 6.17:

	AUX SPI		drivers/spi/spi-bcm2835aux.c -- every CNTL0,
			CNTL1 and STAT field matched; four names did
			not, and 0013 fixes those.
	I2S/PCM		sound/soc/bcm/bcm2835-i2s.c -- every CS_A,
			MODE_A and channel field matched, including
			that CHWID holds the width less 8.
	DMA		drivers/dma/bcm2835-dma.c -- the control block
			layout, every TI and CS bit, and the global
			enable at 0xff0.
	clock manager	drivers/clk/bcm/clk-bcm2835.c -- the 0x5a
			password, CM_PCMCTL/DIV at 0x098/0x09c,
			CM_PWMCTL/DIV at 0x0a0/0x0a4, KILL bit 5 and
			BUSY bit 7, and the kill-then-wait sequence.
	PWM		drivers/pwm/pwm-bcm2835.c -- enable at bit 0,
			mark/space at bit 7, channel 2 eight bits up.
	watchdog	drivers/watchdog/bcm2835_wdt.c -- the register
			offsets and SECS_TO_WDOG_TICKS(x) ((x) << 16),
			which is the 65536Hz tick 0001 models.
	mini UART	drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_bcm2835aux.c --
			CNTL at 0x60 with RXEN 1 and TXEN 2, which is
			the 0x3 reset value 0008 restores.
	BSC slave	the BCM2835 ARM Peripherals datasheet, section
			11, which specifies every bit of CR, FR, RSR,
			DR, IFLS and IMSC.  No Linux driver exists for
			it, upstream or in the vendor tree, which is
			what made it look undocumented.
	SMI		the Raspberry Pi kernel's
			include/linux/broadcom/bcm2835_smi.h, which
			documents what every bit does rather than
			naming it.  Upstream Linux has no driver, which
			is what made this block look undocumented.
	thermal		arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2837.dtsi --
			coefficients <(-538) 412000>.  Note the chip:
			bcm2835 and bcm2836 use 407000, and a Pi 3B is
			a bcm2837.

One model could not be checked this way.  Linux does not drive the
BCM2835 ARM timer at all -- there is no driver and no device tree node
for it, because the system timer and the ARM generic timer are used
instead -- so 0009's 23-bit mode, pre-divider and free-running counter
rest on the datasheet alone.  That is also why the guest-side check in
armtmrbcm.c measures both counters against an independent clock rather
than trusting either.

WHERE QEMU IS MORE LENIENT THAN THE BOARD

This section is the result of a deliberate audit, not a list of things
noticed in passing.  The question asked of each device was: what does
the guest driver do that this model cannot falsify?  Anywhere the
answer was "something", the follow-up was whether patching the model
would make it falsifiable, and at what cost.

Four answers came back, and they are not all the same kind.

	- sd, bus width: a real gap, and closed by patch 0017.  The
	  cost was one new bus call and a comparison; the benefit is
	  that a driver bug which corrupts every block on a real card
	  now fails a boot.  This is what the audit was looking for.

	- sdhci, DMA: NOT a gap.  This entry previously said it was,
	  and was wrong.  Recorded below with the correction, because
	  a claimed divergence that is not one is worse than an
	  unrecorded one -- it invites work that cannot pay.

	- pl011, transmit FIFO: a real gap, and closed by patch 0018
	  after this audit first rejected it.  The rejection was that
	  pacing the drain against a clock changes behaviour for every
	  board sharing the device.  That objection does not survive
	  making the behaviour opt-in per board, which is how capareg
	  and sd-spec-version already work on this machine; once the
	  default is the old behaviour, nothing but the Pi moves.  The
	  entry below is kept in full, with what it got wrong marked,
	  because the reasoning that stopped the work for two rounds is
	  worth more on the page than a clean summary of the result.

	- the firmware clock table against the clock tree: a fifth
	  kind, which this audit did not have a category for and now
	  does.  Nothing here is lenient and nothing is missing; two
	  parts of the model simply disagree, and a guest doing the
	  correct thing is punished for it.  A driver that hardcodes
	  UARTCLK is unaffected; a driver that asks the firmware, which
	  is the only way to be right across Pi models, is out by
	  sixteen.  Closed by 0019.  Worth looking for more of: the
	  question to ask a model is not only "what will it accept"
	  but "does it tell the guest the truth about itself".

	  Asking it of the rest of the firmware clock table turned up
	  one more, recorded below and deliberately NOT patched: the
	  EMMC clock.  The difference between recording it and fixing
	  it is whether anything in the model is paced by the number.
	  The UART's was -- the console had a measurable drain rate
	  after 0018, the boot time moved, and a trace named the
	  cause.  The EMMC's is not: QEMU's SDHCI times no transfer,
	  so 50 against 52 changes nothing the model does and a patch
	  would be moving a constant to please a reader.

	- command timing generally: a real gap, not patched.  Unlike
	  the FIFO it has no compensation on either side and is simply
	  a limit: no model in this series claims a service time.

	- the twenty-two models in the series at the time of that
	  audit: checked for accepting writes the datasheet forbids.
	  0023 and 0024 came after it and were written to the same
	  standard rather than swept by it.  Each already carries
	  LOG_GUEST_ERROR coverage on its own error paths -- the I2S
	  FIFO reports TXERR on overflow and RXERR on reading empty,
	  the SMI reports its address and timing errors, the AUX SPI
	  and the BSC slave both report theirs.  Nothing was added:
	  writing the I2S FIFO while the block is disabled looks like
	  a candidate until the datasheet says pre-loading before
	  setting TXON is the documented technique, and a model that
	  rejected it would be stricter than the part rather than
	  truer to it.

THE SHAPE THIS KEPT TAKING.  Four of the findings above are the same
bug wearing different hardware, and naming it is worth more than the
four fixes, because it is a question anybody can ask of any driver:

	the device is asked to describe itself, and the answer is
	discarded.

	- usbdwc.c read the DWC2 FIFO RAM size out of GHWCFG3 and
	  ended the sequence with USED(n, ptx) -- the compiler being
	  told to stop complaining about a variable nothing looks at
	  -- while laying out three FIFOs to a fixed plan.
	- dmabcm.c did not ask which DMA channels were the ARM's and
	  took channel 0, which is the VideoCore's.
	- sdhcibcm.c took its SD base clock from a capabilities field
	  that reads zero on the real part, and had a guard for that
	  which would have silently left the card at 400kHz.
	- uartvirtarm64.c never programmed the baud divisors at all,
	  so uart->baud reported a rate nothing had set.
	- usbdwc.c on the Pi 4 registered its handler on GPU IRQ 9,
	  the Pi 3B's number for the same core.  9 is a legal INTID
	  on a GIC-400 too -- an SGI nobody raises -- so intrenable()
	  took it, and the root port's connect-detect interrupt went
	  nowhere while the QEMU monitor showed the hub and the
	  network interface plainly attached.

The last of those is from a second board, which is what makes this a
pattern rather than one board's habit: the same shape appears wherever
a number is legal in two contexts and means something different in
each.

Every one of them worked perfectly under emulation, because a model
that does not enforce a constraint cannot punish a driver for ignoring
it.  Every one is a fault on hardware, and three of the four fail
silently there -- corrupted data or a wrong rate, not an error.

The tell is syntactic and easy to grep for: a value read from a device
and then not used in a comparison.  USED() and a discarded return are
the loudest forms.  A driver that asks and does not look is worse than
one that never asked, because the code reads as though it checked.

The entries below give each in full.

	pl011		FIXED by 0018.  QEMU's PL011 modelled no transmit
			FIFO.  PL011_FLAG_TXFF was assigned in exactly
			one place in hw/char/pl011.c, where it is
			cleared, and never set; every write was handed
			straight to the chardev by
			qemu_chr_fe_write_all() under an "XXX this
			blocks entire thread" comment.  So FR.TXFF
			never read 1, a driver that fills until the
			FIFO is full never stopped filling, and the
			transmit-interrupt path that a real board
			depends on to resume a long write was dead code
			under the emulator.

			WHAT THIS ENTRY GOT WRONG, twice.  It said
			modelling the FIFO means pacing the drain
			against a clock, which would change console
			timing for virt, vexpress, raspi, cubieboard
			and the rest and put the qtest suite at risk,
			and that upstream's XXX has stood for the same
			reason.  Every clause of that is true and the
			conclusion still does not follow, because it
			assumes the new behaviour must be unconditional.
			A property defaulting to the old behaviour
			costs the other boards nothing -- this machine
			was already setting capareg and sd-spec-version
			exactly that way, two devices over.  The
			argument was sound and the frame was wrong,
			which is the harder kind to catch.

			The driver side used to compensate:
			uartvirtarm64.c stopped its fill loop at
			sixteen rather than running the queue dry, to
			force the FIFO-full case.  With the FIFO
			modelled that cap is gone and the loop stops on
			TXFF and nothing else, which is the loop that
			runs on the board.

			What the model now buys, and the driver-side
			cap never could: deleting the transmit-interrupt
			continuation from uartvirtarm64.c stops the
			console dead part-way through a line -- 51 lines
			of 99, truncated mid-word.  Against the
			unpatched model the same deletion changes
			nothing at all.  Cost, measured on a raspi3b
			boot to the last self-test marker: 15.6 s
			before, 17.7 s after.

			It also caught a live bug the moment it landed.
			virtarm64_putc(), the path panic() and iprint()
			use, wrote UARTDR blind and then spun a fixed
			count, which was safe only because TXFF could
			never assert; on a Pi that is a dropped
			character in exactly the situation where the
			output matters most.  It now waits for room.
			See the console chapter of
			doc/raspi3b-completion-plan.ms.

	bcm2835-property
			FIXED by 0019.  GET_CLOCK_RATE for the UART
			returned RPI_FIRMWARE_UART_CLK_RATE, 3000000 in
			include/hw/arm/raspi_platform.h, which is a Pi 1
			value.  bcm2835_peripherals.c wires the PL011's
			clk to the CPRMAN's uart-out, 47999625 Hz on
			this machine.  Both numbers are in the same
			tree and they differ by sixteen.

			This one was found by consequence rather than
			by reading: after 0018 the console had a
			measurable drain rate, so pacing it wrongly
			showed up as a boot that took 32 s instead of
			18 s, and the pl011_baudrate_change trace named
			the cause.  Before 0018 the model transmitted
			write-through and a sixteen-fold baud error had
			no observable effect at all.  Accuracy compounds:
			the first patch is what made the second finding
			possible.

			The guest side changed too, and had to.
			virtarm64_baud() was a stub returning 0, so the
			divisors were never written and the part ran at
			whatever the firmware left -- which is right by
			luck on a Pi and hid this entirely.  It now asks
			the mailbox for clock id 2 and programs
			IBRD/FBRD, and Uart.freq no longer carries the
			24 MHz -M virt apb-pclk that had been copied in
			with the driver.

	bcm2835-property, EMMC
			RECORDED, not patched.  GET_CLOCK_RATE for
			clock id 1 answers RPI_FIRMWARE_EMMC_CLK_RATE,
			50000000.  The SDHCI capabilities register on
			the same machine reports its base clock as 52
			MHz -- capareg 0x52134b4, bits 8-15 -- so the
			two sources a driver can ask disagree by 4%.
			That is small and it is not harmless: with a 50
			MHz ceiling, 50 undivided is legal and 52 is
			not, so the same driver lands on 50 MHz or 26
			MHz depending purely on which it asked.

			Not patched because nothing in the model is
			paced by either number.  hw/sd/sdhci.c times no
			transfer against SDCLK, so no value here is
			falsifiable by anything a guest can observe,
			and the fix would be moving a constant for the
			benefit of a human reader.  Recorded so the
			next person to see 26 MHz in a log knows why.

			There is a real leniency underneath it, and it
			belongs to the capabilities register rather
			than to the firmware.  A BCM2835 does not report
			a usable base clock in capabilities at all --
			Linux marks brcm,bcm2835-sdhci missing_caps in
			drivers/mmc/host/sdhci-iproc.c and substitutes
			a table that sets no base-clock field either --
			so on the board that source does not exist and
			the firmware is the only one.  QEMU offering 52
			there lets a driver that reads only capabilities
			work under emulation and stall at the 400 kHz
			identification clock on hardware.

			Not patched either, and this one is a judgement
			rather than an absence of payoff: a base-clock
			field is 0 or it is not, so unlike
			tx-fifo-depth there is no default that leaves
			other guests alone, and every guest booting
			raspi3b would get it.  The driver side handles
			it instead -- sdhcibcm.c asks capabilities, then
			the firmware, and names the source in its marker
			-- and the fallback was verified by zeroing
			capareg bits 8-15 locally (0x52100b4) and
			booting.  See the SDHCI chapter of
			doc/raspi3b-completion-plan.ms.

	bcm2835-dma	RECORDED, not patched, and the guest was the one
			at fault.  The model runs any of the sixteen DMA
			channels for anybody who asks.  The firmware's
			GET_DMA_CHANNELS says 0x3c -- channels 2 to 5 --
			because the VideoCore keeps the rest, and on the
			board a transfer on a channel the firmware also
			uses corrupts whichever side loses, with nothing
			reporting an error.

			Nothing to patch: a model refusing channels the
			firmware has not granted would be inventing an
			enforcement the silicon does not have either.
			The part does not stop you; the firmware simply
			owns the channel.  So the fix is entirely on the
			driver side -- dmabcm.c asks and takes the
			lowest granted channel below 7 -- and unlike the
			SD base clock it needs no patched model to
			verify, since the firmware already answers 0x3c
			and the driver visibly moves from channel 0 to
			channel 2 on an ordinary boot.

	bcm2835-property, the rest of it
			SWEPT, and this is the whole result, so that
			nobody repeats it.  Every remaining tag that
			tells a guest something about the machine was
			compared against the model behind it.

			Consistent, checked, nothing to do: GET_ARM_MEMORY
			and GET_VC_MEMORY both come from the framebuffer
			device's own vcram_base/vcram_size, so the split
			a guest is told is the split that exists.
			GET_BOARD_REVISION cannot disagree with the RAM
			size either -- raspi.c refuses to start with
			"Invalid RAM size" when -m does not match what
			the revision code encodes.  GET_TEMPERATURE says
			25000 millidegrees and bcm2835_thermal.c reports
			the ADC reading for 25C, the same number twice.
			GET_THROTTLED says nothing is throttled, and
			nothing is.

			Untrue but unobservable, recorded and not
			patched: GET_CLOCK_STATE answers 0x1 for every
			clock, so a guest is told every clock is on
			whatever it has done, and SET_CLOCK_STATE is
			LOG_UNIMP, so it cannot turn one off to find
			out.  GET_MAX_CLOCK_RATE and GET_MIN_CLOCK_RATE
			share the GET_CLOCK_RATE case, so maximum,
			minimum and current are one number and a guest
			choosing a rate between the bounds has exactly
			one choice.  Patch 0019 left that collapse alone
			-- it changed which number all three report for
			the UART, not that all three are the same -- and
			it should not be read as having fixed max/min
			semantics.  Neither is patched because no model
			behaviour depends on either answer: there is
			nothing a guest could do differently that the
			machine would notice.

	dwc2		FIXED by 0020, and it is the fifth category again
			-- the model describing itself wrongly -- with a
			twist: here the wrong description was not a
			stale constant but an arithmetic accident.  The
			GHWCFG registers exist for no other purpose than
			to be read by a guest; hcd-dwc2.c writes them at
			reset and never reads them back.  So a wrong
			value changes nothing the model does, and there
			is no test anywhere that could have caught it.
			It is found by reading, or not at all.

			The guest side had the matching fault, and it is
			the more instructive one.  usbdwc.c read the
			core's FIFO depth out of GHWCFG3 and then threw
			it away -- literally, the line was USED(n, ptx)
			-- while laying out three FIFOs to a fixed plan.
			Asking a device how big it is and then not
			looking at the answer is worse than not asking:
			it reads as though the layout were checked.  It
			is checked now, and the check was verified by
			building a QEMU that reports 1024 words instead
			of 4096, which panics with
			"fifo layout 774+256+512 words needs 1542, the
			core reports 1024".  The model was restored
			afterwards; no patch ships for it.

	sdhci		NOT a divergence, and this entry used to say it
			was.  The claim here was that the model is more
			capable than the part, because hw/sd/sdhci.c
			implements SDMA and ADMA.  It does implement
			them, but this machine never offers them: the
			raspi capareg is 0x52134b4 (bcm2835_peripherals.c),
			whose SDMA bit 22 and ADMA2 bit 19 are both
			zero, and sdhci.c:1238 strips SDHC_TRNS_DMA
			from any write when capareg says no SDMA.  A
			DMA data path here would not have "passed every
			check"; it could not have run at all.

			Reading the register from the guest confirms
			it: base 52MHz, maxblk 1024, hs 1, sdma 0,
			adma2 0, v33 1 -- which is also what Linux's
			bcm2835_data substitutes when it declares the
			real register untrustworthy (missing_caps).
			Model and part agree.

			Two quirks the emulator does still hide, both
			from Linux's brcm,bcm2835-sdhci entry:
			SDHCI_QUIRK_NO_HISPD_BIT, so the host's
			Control0 bit 2 must not be set even though the
			standard sequence pairs it with CMD6 (sdhcibcm.c
			says so in a comment, because adding it is the
			tidy-looking change), and
			SDHCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_CARD_DETECTION, which is the
			mechanism the -DSDROUTETEST diagnostic reads.

	sd		CLOSED by patch 0017.  The model did not
			enforce bus width: ACMD6 recorded it in
			sd_status[0] bits 7:6 and nothing read it
			again, while the host's side was never
			communicated at all -- sdhci wrote hostctl1 and
			the card never heard.  So a host that set its
			four-bit bit without ever telling the card
			transferred exactly as if it had done
			everything right, and a driver bug that
			corrupts every block on a real card passed
			silently.

			0017 pushes the host's width to the card and
			refuses a transfer when the two disagree.  The
			guest marker is SDHCI-4BIT-OK rather than
			-ACCEPTED because the sequence is now falsifiable:
			deleting the ACMD6 while still setting Control0
			gives "SDHCI-4BIT-FAIL SD_STATUS did not
			arrive" and fails every later block read.

			What is still not shown, and is not showable,
			is that four lines are electrically driven.
			The model has no wire to narrow.  What 0017
			buys is the sequence and the agreement, which
			is the half a driver can get wrong.

THE PATCHES

0001 bcm2835_powermgt: model the watchdog countdown.

	Stock QEMU resets the machine the instant a guest sets RSTC's
	full-reset bit, and logs PM_WDOG as unimplemented.  The
	watchdog therefore cannot be armed, watched, fed or stopped,
	and os/inferno/raspi3b/pmbcm.c's arm-and-stop self-test
	reboot-loops against it.

	With the patch the counter ticks at 65536 Hz, counts only
	while RSTC selects full-reset, reads back the time remaining,
	and freezes where it stopped.  Linux's reboot path is
	unaffected: it arms with 10 ticks, about 150us.

0002 bcm2835_gpio: honour alt3 on the SD card pins.

	Stock QEMU recognises only alt0 (SDHOST) on GPIO 48-53.  The
	alt3 write that hands the card to the Arasan SDHCI block --
	what sdhcibcm.c does, and what the hardware requires -- is
	ignored, so the card stays wherever it was parented.  Since it
	is parented to SDHCI at reset, the SDHCI driver appears to
	work while its routing write does nothing, and a driver that
	omitted the write entirely would pass identically.

	With the patch alt3 selects SDHCI, so the routing is real: the
	card can be handed to SDHOST and taken back.

0003 hw/usb: model the SMSC LAN9514 ethernet controller.

	The Pi's onboard network adapter.  Stock qemu offers usb-net,
	which is CDC ECM, and nothing that answers the vendor requests
	and framing an smsc95xx-style driver uses, so the SMSC half of
	appl/cmd/usb/etherusb.b -- about a thousand lines -- had never
	executed.

	The model covers the ethernet function: vendor requests A0/A1
	for register access, the transmit command words, the receive
	status word and FCS, burst packing under MEF and the burst
	cap, and the internal PHY at address 1.  No EEPROM is fitted,
	as on a Pi, so a driver falls back to the firmware's MAC.

	Run it with:

		-device usb-lan9514,netdev=n0 -netdev user,id=n0

0004 hw/sd: model a generic SDIO function.

	qemu implements SD and eMMC memory cards and no SDIO at all,
	so sdhcibcm.c's sdiocmd52 and sdiocmd53 -- Phase 6's transport
	-- were unrunnable rather than merely untested.

	sd-io is an I/O-only function: CCCR at function 0, one I/O
	function behind it, and CMD5/CMD3/CMD7/CMD52/CMD53.  It
	answers nothing else -- CMD8, ACMD41 and CMD9 are absent on
	purpose, so a memory-card probe is refused rather than
	indulged.

	It is deliberately generic and not a BCM43430.  There is no
	ether4330 in this tree and no firmware loader, so a Wi-Fi
	model would have no consumer and would prove only that the two
	halves agree with each other.

0005 hw/gpio/bcm2835_gpio: place an I/O function, and never displace one.

	The card routing assumed the only device on these buses was
	the card.  With an SDIO function present it would reparent the
	function as though it were the card, breaking it and stranding
	the real card.  Move only a removable card, find it wherever
	it is, and refuse a bus that already holds a device; settle a
	non-card device onto the Arasan, which is the only controller
	this SoC wires one to.

	Run it with:

		-device sd-io

	The function needs no bus argument: it is settled onto the
	Arasan on the guest's first pin programming.  Attaching it
	gives the wiring a real Pi 3B has -- card on SDHOST, Wi-Fi on
	the Arasan -- so SDHCI-NONE is then the correct result, not a
	failure.

0006 hw/misc/bcm2835_cprman: match a clock mux's divisor register.

	A real qemu bug, found by writing the PWM driver.
	update_mux_from_cm() matches a mux's CM_DIV at cm_offset + 4,
	but cm_offset is a word index and CTL/DIV are adjacent words,
	so "+ 4" names the CTL register of the mux two slots on.
	CM_PWMCTL (word 40) is matched by the OTP mux's 36 + 4, so
	programming the PWM clock updated the OTP mux and returned and
	the PWM clock never started.  Affects every mux, not just PWM.

0007 hw/misc: model the BCM2835 PWM.

	PWM_OFFSET was defined and nothing was mapped there, so a
	guest reached unassigned memory.  Two channels, range and data
	registers, the shared 16-word FIFO with full/empty, the status
	register, and the serialiser clock taken from the clock
	manager's "pwm" mux so a channel enabled without a clock is
	reported rather than silently idle.

	No serialiser runs: nothing consumes the FIFO on its own and
	no waveform reaches a pin, so GAPO never comes on by itself
	and a duty cycle cannot be measured.  The registers, the FIFO
	discipline and the error reporting are modelled, which is the
	part a driver gets wrong.

	Needs no command-line argument; the block is part of the SoC.

0008 hw/char/bcm2835_aux: keep the mini UART's storage registers.

	A real qemu bug.  LCR, MCR, CNTL, SCRATCH and the baud rate
	divisor were decoded and discarded, so a driver could not read
	back what it had just written and had no way to tell a mini
	UART it had configured from one it had not.  MSR now reports
	CTS and DSR asserted, CNTL resets to 0x3 as the datasheet
	says, and a 7-bit LCR selection is reported rather than
	silently ignored.

	Needs no command-line argument, but the mini UART is a second
	serial port, so seeing its output takes a second -serial:

		-serial mon:stdio -serial file:aux.log

	Without that the AUX result still appears on the console --
	auxbcm.c reports through print() -- but nothing written to the
	mini UART itself is visible anywhere.

0009 hw/timer: model the BCM2835 ARM timer.

	create_unimp(... "bcm2835-sp804" ...) mapped nothing, and the
	datasheet is explicit that this is not an SP804: the counter
	is 23-bit rather than 32, there is a pre-divider between the
	APB clock and the counter, and there is a second free-running
	up-counter with its own prescaler.  A stock SP804 driver gets
	the countdown roughly right and the free-running counter not
	at all.

0010 hw/timer: compute the ARM timer counters from the clock.

	A bug in 0009, kept separate because the reasoning generalises.
	Driving the countdown with a ptimer and reporting
	ptimer_get_count() for VALUE reads back a hard zero for any
	access taken after the counter expired but before ptimer's
	callback ran -- hw/core/ptimer.c clamps to 0 to "prevent timer
	underflowing if it should already have triggered", whatever
	the policy flags say.  Under TCG a guest in a calibration loop
	starves the main loop, so that window is milliseconds wide,
	and a guest polling VALUE saw the counter parked at zero
	instead of wrapping.

	Both counters are now computed on demand from the virtual
	clock and a plain QEMUTimer delivers only the interrupt, so
	the value and the deadline come from one base and cannot
	drift.  include/hw/ptimer.h says new devices should not use
	PTIMER_POLICY_LEGACY; this one uses no ptimer at all.

0011 hw/audio: model the BCM2835 I2S/PCM interface.

	I2S_OFFSET was another create_unimp, so a guest could neither
	set a sample rate nor move a frame.  Both 64-word FIFOs, the
	frame and channel configuration, the thresholds, the sync
	flags, the write-one-to-clear error bits, the DMA request
	levels and the interrupt, clocked from the clock manager's pcm
	mux.  The FIFOs are advanced on demand for the reason 0010
	gives.

	No audio backend: TX data is discarded and RX reads zeroes,
	which is what an unconnected DIN pin gives on a board.

	One deliberate departure from the datasheet, and it is in a
	comment in the model too: its TXTHR and RXTHR description is
	defective -- four settings, but the text for 01 and 10 is
	identical and omits the fraction.  The hardware uses quarters.

	Needs no command-line argument; the block is part of the SoC.

0012 hw/char/bcm2835_aux: model the two AUX SPI masters.

	The AUX block holds three peripherals behind one AUX_ENABLES
	register, and only the mini UART was modelled; 0x80 and 0xc0
	fell through to "Bad offset".  Not a second copy of
	hw/ssi/bcm2835_spi.c: the shift length is 1 to 32 BITS rather
	than a whole number of bytes, and transmitted data comes from
	the top of the written word while received data arrives at the
	bottom.

	The IO and TXHOLD windows differ in whether chip select drops
	after the word, and modelling that was necessary rather than
	decorative: an m25p16 answered a JEDEC identify with zeroes
	until CS actually fell and rose, because nothing else can
	resynchronise a peripheral's command state machine.

	Attach a peripheral to exercise it:

		-drive if=none,format=raw,id=mtd0,file=flash.img \
		-device m25p16,bus=aux-spi1,cs=0,drive=mtd0

	which gives "auxspi: jedec 20 20 15".  That result is the
	strongest in the series: the flash is qemu's own model, so the
	register layer is checked against something this port did not
	write.

	No serialiser runs, as with SPI0: a transfer completes inside
	the register write that starts it, so TX_FULL never comes on
	by itself.  Variable-width mode is logged, not modelled.

0013 hw/char/bcm2835_aux: use Linux'''s names for four CNTL0 bits.

	The values in 0012 were written from memory of
	drivers/spi/spi-bcm2835aux.c and then checked against it;
	every one matched.  Four names did not.  No functional change.

EXERCISING THE WHOLE BOARD

The markers a boot prints depend on what is attached.  With nothing on
the command line the SD, USB-storage and ethernet paths report -NONE,
which is correct and is not a failure.  Everything at once:

	qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi3b -m 1G -nographic -kernel iraspi3b \
		-drive file=sd.img,if=sd,format=raw \
		-device usb-lan9514,netdev=n0 -netdev user,id=n0 \
		-drive if=none,format=raw,id=mtd0,file=flash.img \
		-device m25p16,bus=aux-spi1,cs=0,drive=mtd0 \
		-drive if=none,id=stick,format=raw,file=fat.img \
		-device usb-storage,drive=stick \
		-serial mon:stdio -serial file:aux.log

fat.img is a real FAT16 image, built as below; that invocation reports
every marker OK apart from USB-MOUSE-NONE, which is correct because the
mouse is not in the line.

The mouse is not in that line, deliberately.  A boot-protocol mouse
reports nothing until something moves it, so attaching -device usb-mouse
and leaving it alone gives USB-MOUSE-NONE -- correct, since no events
arrived, but it reads as "no mouse attached".  The check needs events
injected, which needs the monitor on a socket:

	qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi3b -m 1G -nographic -kernel iraspi3b \
		-drive file=sd.img,if=sd,format=raw \
		-device usb-mouse -monitor unix:/tmp/mon.sock,server,nowait

then, once the log shows "usbd: ep3.0: boot mouse on ep3.1", send over
that socket:

	mouse_move 40 40      (several times)
	mouse_button 2        then mouse_button 0
	mouse_button 4        then mouse_button 0

and the board reports USB-MOUSE-OK with buttons 6.

Note the button numbers.  The monitor's own help for mouse_button says
"1=L, 2=M, 4=R" and is wrong: include/ui/console.h defines
MOUSE_EVENT_LBUTTON 1, MOUSE_EVENT_RBUTTON 2 and MOUSE_EVENT_MBUTTON 4,
so 2 is right and 4 is middle.  The check wants both, because HID and
devpointer transpose right and middle and a motion-only test cannot see
that.

The USB stick is worth a note.  DISK-NONE means no mass storage was
attached; DOS-FAIL means one was, with no filesystem on it.  A plain
zeroed image gives the second.

There are two ways to give it a filesystem, and they do not test the
same thing.

qemu's own vvfat -- file=fat:rw:<directory> -- presents a host
directory as a FAT16 volume.  It is the convenient one and it is what
the everything-at-once line above uses, but it reads only: the board
reports DISK-BLK-OK, DISK-DOS-OK and DISK-READ-OK, and then
DISK-WRITE-FAIL, because vvfat's write support is experimental and
does not carry these writes.  The failure is at the reopen, "no such
fid", with the create and the write both having reported success --
which is the shape of a write that went nowhere, not of one that was
refused.

A real FAT image gets DISK-WRITE-OK.  This README used to say macOS
could not make one, because newfs_msdos on a plain file fails with
"Cannot get partition offset".  It can; the file needs a device node
first:

	dd if=/dev/zero of=fat.img bs=1m count=16
	dev=$(hdiutil attach -nomount \
		-imagekey diskimage-class=CRawDiskImage fat.img)
	newfs_msdos -F 16 -v PIDISK $dev
	mount -t msdos $dev /mnt && ... && umount /mnt
	hdiutil detach $dev

then boot with -drive if=none,id=stick,format=raw,file=fat.img and
-device usb-storage,drive=stick.  The board reports DISK-WRITE-OK, and
re-attaching the image afterwards shows PI.TXT on it, 29 bytes,
"written by inferno on the pi" -- the guest's bytes on a filesystem
the host reads back independently.  That is the whole write path:
dossrv, SCSI WRITE(10), the bulk endpoints and the DWC2 driver.

One wart shows up in that listing.  PI.TXT is dated 2097.  The Pi has
no RTC, the kernel's clock starts at the epoch, and FAT counts from
1980, so a 1970 timestamp has nowhere to go.  It is cosmetic, it is in
shared dossrv rather than in this board, and it is left alone.

SELF-TESTS

Two kernel self-tests are compiled out by default, because each can
leave the board unusable if the hardware does not behave -- on a real
Pi as much as under an emulator -- and a boot-time check that can brick
its own boot is not worth having on by default.

	-DWDOGSELFTEST	pmbcm.c: arm the watchdog, watch it count
			down, stop it.  If the stop does not take, the
			machine reboots 8 seconds later, and again
			after that.

	-DSDROUTETEST	sdhcibcm.c: route the card to SDHOST and
			back.  If it cannot be taken back, the board
			has no SD card.

	-DSDIOTEST	sdhcibcm.c: bring up an SDIO function and move
			data through it with CMD52 and CMD53, in byte
			mode and block mode.  Bringing SDIO up resets
			the Arasan, which an SD card sitting on it
			would not enjoy.

Build them by compiling the two files by hand with the define, then
relinking:

	cd os/inferno/raspi3b
	mk
	7c -wFV -DWDOGSELFTEST -DSDROUTETEST ... pmbcm.c sdhcibcm.c
	touch -t 203001010000 pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7
	mk

The touch is needed because mk rebuilds an object whose timestamp
merely ties with its source, and the two are written in the same
second.  It also dates the objects to 2030, so mk will never rebuild
them again.  Delete them to get back to a default kernel:

	rm -f pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7
	mk

Deleting the objects is not always enough on its own: mk compares the
kernel against them, and with them gone it can decide there is nothing
to do and leave the old kernel in place.  If iraspi3b still has the
self-test strings in it, force the issue:

	rm -f pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7 main.7 iraspi3b
	touch pmbcm.c sdhcibcm.c main.c
	mk
	strings iraspi3b | grep -c SDIO-OK	# expect 0

Do not leave a self-test kernel installed in Inferno/aarch64/bin: it
reboot-loops on stock qemu, which is a confusing artifact to leave for
someone else.

CHANGING LIMBO

A change to a .b under appl/ does not reach the kernel by rebuilding
the kernel: the board's root image is built from the installed .dis
files, and the image is only regenerated when it is missing.  So:

	cd appl/cmd/usb && mk etherusb.dis
	cp etherusb.dis $ROOT/dis/usb/
	cd $ROOT/os/inferno/raspi3b
	rm -f raspi3b.root.s raspi3b.root.7
	mk

Skipping either the copy or the rm leaves the old code running, which
looks exactly like a change that did not work.

What they print, against patched qemu with a 16M raw SD image
(-drive file=sd.img,if=sd,format=raw):

	WDOG-PRESENT loaded and holding while disarmed
	WDOG-OK armed, counted down and stopped
	sdhci: alt0 released the card, alt3 took it back
	SDROUTE-OK
	SDHCI-OK

With -DSDIOTEST and -device sd-io, on the Pi wiring (card on SDHOST,
I/O function on the Arasan):

	sdio: 1 I/O function(s), rca 0x...
	sdio: CCCR revision 0x32
	SDIO-OK CMD52 and CMD53 carried data through function 1, byte and block mode
	SDHCI-NONE (no /dev/sdH0/data: file does not exist)
	SD-OK

SDHCI-NONE is the correct result there, not a failure: the Arasan has
the I/O function, so the card stays on SDHOST.

Against stock qemu the watchdog test reboot-loops and never reaches a
banner, and the routing test reports SDROUTE-FAIL.  The default kernel,
with neither define, boots clean on both.

The PWM needs no define either.  A default kernel on patched qemu
prints:

	pwm: pwm at 0x3f20c000, serialiser clock 200000 Hz
	PWM-OK clock, mark/space, balanced, FIFO full/empty and errors

and on stock qemu prints PWM-NONE and carries on, the block being
genuinely absent there.

The LAN9514 needs no define.  With patched qemu and the device
attached, a default kernel prints:

	etherusb: smsc id_rev 0xec000002
	ETHERUSB serving /net.alt/ether mac 525400123456 (SMSC LAN95xx ep3.0)
	ETHERBIND-OK
	ETHER-DHCP-OK ip=10.0.2.15 gw=10.0.2.2
	ETHER-PING-DONE
